# Model Card: Credit Card Fraud Detection (MLP)


---

## Model Details

- **Name:** Credit Card Fraud Detection — MLP at Cost-Sensitive Threshold
- **Version:** 1.0 (May 2026)
- **Type:** Binary classifier — Multi-Layer Perceptron (feed-forward neural network)
- **Architecture:** Two hidden layers (32 → 16 units), ReLU activations, Adam optimiser, early stopping
- **Decision rule:** A transaction is flagged as fraud when `predict_proba(fraud) ≥ 0.028`
- **Framework:** scikit-learn 1.x (`MLPClassifier`), wrapped in an `imblearn` pipeline
- **Authors:** Machine Learning Foundations group project, IE University
- **Licence:** Course project — for educational use
- **Contact:** See repository on GitHub

---

## Intended Use

**Primary intended use:** Educational demonstration of an imbalanced binary-classification pipeline for credit-card fraud detection, including data preprocessing, model comparison, cost-sensitive threshold tuning, and interpretability analysis.

**Primary intended users:** Students, instructors, and other practitioners studying applied machine learning, imbalanced-class problems, or cost-sensitive evaluation.

**Out-of-scope uses:** This model is **not** suitable for production deployment in a real payments system. It was trained on a single two-day snapshot of European cardholder data from 2013 with anonymised PCA-transformed features, and has not been validated on any other distribution. Any production deployment would require fresh training data, ongoing monitoring, drift detection, and bias auditing.

---

## Training Data

| | |
|---|---|
| Dataset | ULB Credit Card Fraud Detection (public, available on Kaggle) |
| Period covered | Two days, September 2013 |
| Total records | 284,807 transactions |
| Fraud rate | 0.173% (492 fraud cases) |
| Training split | 199,364 transactions (70%), stratified by class |
| Validation split | 42,721 transactions (15%) — used for model and threshold selection |
| Test split | 42,722 transactions (15%) — used once for final evaluation |

Features `V1`–`V28` are anonymised PCA components produced by the dataset providers to protect cardholder privacy. `Time` was transformed into a cyclical hour-of-day signal (`sin`/`cos`) and `Amount` was transformed by `log1p` to reduce skew. All features were standardised inside the pipeline.

---

## Evaluation Metrics

Because the positive (fraud) class is extremely rare, **accuracy is not used as a primary metric** — a model predicting "legitimate" for every transaction would score 99.83% accuracy while detecting zero fraud. The following metrics were prioritised:

- **PR-AUC** (area under the precision-recall curve) — primary ranking metric for the rare positive class
- **Recall** — fraction of true fraud cases caught
- **Precision** — fraction of fraud alerts that are actually fraud
- **F1-score** — harmonic mean of precision and recall
- **Expected cost** — business-oriented metric using a 1:100 false-positive : false-negative cost ratio
- **ROC-AUC** — reported but de-emphasised due to saturation on imbalanced data

---

## Quantitative Performance

Held-out test set (n = 42,722; 74 actual fraud cases):

| Setting | Recall | Precision | F1 | ROC-AUC | PR-AUC | TP | FN | FP | TN | Total cost |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Default threshold (0.5) | 70.3% | 89.7% | 0.788 | 0.965 | 0.812 | 52 | 22 | 6 | 42,642 | 2,206 |
| **Cost-sensitive (0.028)** | **85.1%** | 43.4% | 0.575 | 0.965 | 0.812 | **63** | **11** | 82 | 42,566 | **1,182** |

The cost-sensitive operating point catches 11 additional frauds compared with the default threshold, at the price of 76 additional false alarms — a trade favoured under the assumed cost ratio.

